<Base title="WAL, checkpoints, and recovery" description="Separate WAL generation, durable commit, data-page writes and checkpoints, then follow what PostgreSQL can reconstruct after a crash.">
 <meta slot="head" name="robots" content="noindex" />
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage · Working draft</p><h1>WAL, checkpoints, and recovery</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>PostgreSQL 18</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">Storage and durability ↗</a></div></header>
  <p>A transaction can change several data pages before it commits. Requiring every one of those pages to reach persistent storage before acknowledging every transaction would tie commit latency to many scattered writes. PostgreSQL instead uses a sequential write-ahead log, or WAL, to preserve the information needed to recover logged changes.</p>
  <p>That creates several moments worth distinguishing: a change is made in memory, a log record is inserted, log bytes reach durable storage, the transaction commits, and changed data pages are written. They are coordinated, but they do not all happen at once. Understanding their order explains both why a commit can survive a crash and why a WAL record is not evidence that an application operation committed.</p>
  <p>We will follow one stock change from three units to one in PostgreSQL 18. The server explanation assumes ordinary logged tables, <code>fsync=on</code>, <code>full_page_writes=on</code>, <code>synchronous_commit=on</code>, reliable storage flushes and no configured synchronous standby unless stated otherwise.</p>
  <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#log">What enters the log</a></li><li><a href="#commit">The commit boundary</a></li><li><a href="#checkpoint">Checkpoints and restart</a></li><li><a href="#settings">Settings change the promise</a></li><li><a href="#recovery">Recovery beyond a crash</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="log"><h2>What enters the log</h2>
   <p>When PostgreSQL updates our stock row, it changes heap-version state in buffers and generates records describing the logged work. WAL also covers changes to indexes and other persistent structures as appropriate. It is a recovery representation of database changes, not a list of original SQL statements or a complete business audit trail.</p>
   <p>WAL records have positions called <strong>log sequence numbers</strong>, or LSNs, offsets in the log stream. PostgreSQL exposes positions for insertion, writing and flushing. Those positions answer different questions: bytes can have been inserted into WAL buffers without yet being durably flushed.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-wal-records">WAL-record lesson</a> creates one row, ID 1 with available three, and saves an earlier WAL insertion position in a temporary marker table. Then it opens a transaction and executes this observed update:</p>
   <Sql code={update} label="Generate WAL inside a transaction that has not committed" />
   <p><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> really executes the UPDATE. The WAL option reports log generation attributed to that statement; the verified run reported one record and 71 bytes. Those exact counts depend on page and checkpoint state, so they are observations rather than a promised cost for every update.</p>
   <p>The following SELECT sees available one because this connection sees its own change. No COMMIT has been sent. Now roll it back and compare the insertion position with the marker:</p>
   <Sql code={rollback} label="Compare logical rollback with log advancement" />
   <p>Available returns to three, while <code>wal_advanced</code> is true. Rollback did not erase the stream. PostgreSQL can log physical work before knowing the transaction’s eventual outcome, and recovery must interpret that work with transaction status.</p>
   <p>The marker comparison measures whether the log position advanced, not the exact bytes caused only by the stock update. Other work can advance a server’s shared WAL stream. The statement-level EXPLAIN counters and stream-position functions are different observations. PostgreSQL describes LSNs in <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-internals.html">WAL internals</a> and the position functions in its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-admin.html#FUNCTIONS-ADMIN-BACKUP">administration functions</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="commit"><h2>The commit boundary</h2>
   <p>Run the update again, this time followed by COMMIT. Under the stated local durability settings, PostgreSQL must make the transaction’s required WAL, including its commit record, durable before acknowledging success. It need not force the changed heap page to storage at that same moment.</p>
   <p>The following is one possible sequence, deliberately choosing a case where the changed heap page is written later. “Stored page” means the persistent copy; “buffered page” means the current memory copy.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="A possible WAL-first commit sequence"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Buffered heap state</th><th>Stored heap state</th><th>Recovery information</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Before update</td><td>Visible stock 3</td><td>Stock 3</td><td>Earlier committed state</td></tr><tr><td>Update runs</td><td>New version with stock 1, transaction pending</td><td>Still earlier state</td><td>Update WAL generated</td></tr><tr><td>Durable commit acknowledged</td><td>New version committed</td><td>May still be earlier state</td><td>Required update and commit WAL durable</td></tr><tr><td>Later page write</td><td>Committed version</td><td>Page contains logged changes</td><td>WAL ordering already satisfied</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>If the server crashes after the acknowledgement but before the later page write, the data page alone is insufficient. The durable log supplies the changes needed to reconstruct the committed result. This is why “the page is still dirty” does not imply “the transaction is not durable”.</p>
   <p>The ordering rule also applies when a page is written before its modifying transaction commits: the WAL required to describe that page change must already be durable. Such a page can contain versions belonging to an uncommitted transaction. PostgreSQL’s visibility machinery prevents those versions from becoming committed application data merely because their bytes reached storage.</p>
   <p>Several transactions can benefit from one log flush covering their required positions. This is one reason sequential WAL can make durable commits efficient compared with forcing many unrelated data pages for each transaction. It does not change the requirement that the acknowledged transaction’s log position be covered by the chosen durability policy.</p>
   <p>The application must still handle a lost commit reply. The server can complete the durable commit and then lose the connection before the caller receives success. WAL ensures recoverability of the server’s result; it does not deliver the missing network response. A stable request identity is needed to reconcile the application outcome.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="checkpoint"><h2>Checkpoints and restart</h2>
   <p>Without a later boundary, restart would need to reconsider an ever-growing history. A <strong>checkpoint</strong> coordinates writing the required dirty pages and records where recovery can begin replaying. The checkpoint’s redo position identifies the necessary starting point, which is not simply “the end of the log when checkpointing finished”.</p>
   <p>Transactions continue changing pages while a checkpoint proceeds. Completing it does not mean every page in memory is clean forever, nor does it commit transactions still in progress. It establishes a recovery boundary consistent with the log and the page writes performed for that checkpoint.</p>
   <p>At restart after a crash, PostgreSQL reads control and checkpoint information, then scans WAL forward from the required redo position. Redo reconstructs logged changes that need to be reflected in data files. A transaction without a successful commit outcome does not become visible merely because redo encounters its page changes. PostgreSQL’s MVCC and transaction-status rules distinguish committed replacements from abandoned versions.</p>
   <p>A further problem is a partially written data page. A database page is usually larger than a storage device’s atomic write unit, so a crash can leave old and new parts mixed. With <code>full_page_writes</code> enabled, PostgreSQL logs a full-page image on the first qualifying modification after a checkpoint, allowing recovery to restore a coherent page before applying subsequent changes. That extra log volume serves a different purpose from an ordinary row-change record.</p>
   <p>Checkpoint frequency therefore affects more than restart duration. More frequent checkpoints can add writes and increase repeated full-page-image logging. Longer intervals can leave more replay work and require more WAL space. PostgreSQL spreads checkpoint writes to reduce bursts; inspect actual write and checkpoint behaviour before changing the settings. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-configuration.html">WAL configuration chapter</a> explains the tradeoff.</p>
   <p>A checkpoint is not a backup. If the storage holding both data and needed WAL is destroyed, knowing that a checkpoint completed does not recreate it elsewhere. Nor does a checkpoint protect against an accidental committed DELETE: that change is valid database history and will be replayed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="settings"><h2>Settings change the promise</h2>
   <p><code>synchronous_commit=off</code> allows PostgreSQL to acknowledge a commit before its WAL is durably flushed. A crash can then lose recently acknowledged transactions while maintaining a consistent database after recovery. That is a changed data-loss promise, not simply a faster implementation of the same promise. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-async-commit.html">asynchronous-commit documentation</a> explains the boundary.</p>
   <p>Disabling <code>fsync</code> is a different change: it removes required storage-ordering guarantees and can leave unrecoverable corruption after a crash. Keeping it on also relies on the operating system and storage truthfully honouring flush requests. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-reliability.html">reliability chapter</a> follows that responsibility through the storage stack.</p>
   <p>The browser lesson’s observed runtime reports <code>synchronous_commit=on</code> and <code>fsync=off</code>. Those settings belong to PGlite’s embedded environment. Seeing COMMIT succeed there confirms the SQL transaction result; it does not demonstrate the server power-loss guarantee described above.</p>
   <p>With synchronous replication configured, a commit may also wait for designated standby progress. The chosen <code>synchronous_commit</code> level distinguishes remote receipt/write, durable remote flush and replay. Without a configured synchronous standby, an “on” setting does not conjure a second durable copy. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-SYNCHRONOUS-COMMIT">setting reference</a> defines those conditions.</p>
   <p>State the required failure domain before selecting the policy. Surviving a process crash with local storage intact, surviving loss of the local disk and surviving a regional outage require different surviving resources. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-failover/">replication and failover article</a> follows the additional acknowledgement and authority decisions.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="recovery"><h2>Recovery beyond a crash</h2>
   <p>Physical backup and continuous archiving extend the same replay mechanism beyond the current data directory. A suitable base backup plus the required uninterrupted WAL sequence can reconstruct later points. Stopping before an accidental transaction can recover an earlier state, but also excludes legitimate later work that must be reconciled.</p>
   <p>Retained WAL has an operating cost. Archiving that cannot keep up, or a replication slot whose consumer no longer advances, can retain enough log to threaten storage capacity. Removing files manually is not a sound repair: those files may still be required for recovery or replication. Establish why they are retained and repair that dependency with a deliberate recovery plan.</p>
   <p>Measure a recovery drill on the actual deployment arrangement. The database becoming available is only one stage; verify acknowledged operations, application permissions, required external state and deletion decisions before reopening the service. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">backup and restore article</a> provides a bounded logical-restore procedure and explains its different scope.</p>
   <p>The stock change now has a precise story. Its update can generate WAL before commit. A durable commit requires the configured log guarantee before acknowledgement. Data pages can follow later. Checkpoints bound the necessary replay work, and restart uses the surviving log and transaction state to reconstruct a consistent result. Each statement describes a different boundary; keeping them separate is what makes the durability promise understandable.</p>
  </section>
